<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> <div><p>At the opening of the <em>Enquiry</em>, Hume distinguishes two types of philosophy. What does he see as the primary weakness of the more popular, literary type?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">It focuses on metaphysics rather than ethics and politics.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">It is inaccessible to most readers because of its technical vocabulary.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">It is entertaining but fails to investigate the principles underlying human thought and conduct.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">It relies too heavily on logical argument and ignores lived experience.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Hume distinguishes between an 'easy and obvious' philosophy that paints virtue in attractive colors and an 'accurate and abstruse' philosophy that investigates the underlying principles of human understanding. The popular type entertains but never digs beneath the surface.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Hume's complaint about the popular, literary type of philosophy is not that it is too logical or too technical, but that it contents itself with surface-level appeals to sentiment without examining the principles that govern thought. Review Section 1 of the Enquiry.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> <div><p>Hume's stated ambition in the <em>Enquiry</em> is modeled on Newton's achievement in physics. What exactly does Hume hope to do for the mind that Newton did for bodies?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Prove mathematically that the mind exists independently of the body.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Establish that God is necessary to explain the regularity of thought, just as Newton's laws require a divine creator.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Demonstrate that mental events obey the same physical laws as matter.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Discover a small set of principles that govern the operations of the understanding, just as Newton discovered laws governing motion.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Just as Newton reduced the motions of all physical bodies to a few simple laws, Hume hopes to discover a small number of general principles that explain all the operations of the human mind — an ambitious program he calls the 'science of man.'</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Hume does not aim to prove the mind's independence, demonstrate that mental events obey physical laws, or establish God's existence. His goal is descriptive: to find the simple principles behind all mental operations, just as Newton found laws behind physical motion. Review Section 1.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> <div><p>According to Hume, what is the primary difference between impressions and ideas?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Impressions are true; ideas can be false.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Impressions are forceful and vivid; ideas are fainter copies of impressions.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Impressions are caused by external objects; ideas are caused by internal reflection.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Impressions are simple; ideas can be complex.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The distinction between impressions and ideas rests entirely on their degree of force and vivacity. Impressions — sensations, passions, emotions as they first appear — are lively and forceful; ideas are the faint images of impressions in thinking and reasoning.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Hume's distinction is not about where perceptions come from, their complexity, or their truth value. It is about intensity: impressions strike the mind with greater force and vivacity than the fainter copies we call ideas. Review the Impressions and Ideas lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> <div><p>Hume's Copy Principle states that every simple idea is a copy of a corresponding simple impression. What is the philosophical significance of this principle?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">It provides a test for whether a philosophical concept is meaningful: if no impression corresponds to it, the concept is suspect.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">It establishes that all human beings share the same fundamental experiences.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">It proves that the external world exists independently of the mind.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">It shows that complex ideas are more reliable than simple ones.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The Copy Principle functions as an empiricist test for meaning: if you cannot trace a philosophical concept back to some original impression, the concept is suspect and may be empty. This principle becomes Hume's most powerful critical tool throughout the Enquiry.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The Copy Principle does not prove the external world exists or rank complex ideas above simple ones. Its philosophical significance is as a criterion of meaning — a way to test whether any concept has genuine content by asking what impression it copies. Review the Impressions and Ideas lecture.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> <div><p>Hume acknowledges that the imagination can form ideas of things never directly experienced — for example, a golden mountain. How does he account for this without abandoning the Copy Principle?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">He denies that we truly have an idea of a golden mountain — we merely have the words for it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Complex ideas like "golden mountain" are assembled from simple ideas (gold, mountain) each of which does copy an original impression.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">He argues that we must have experienced golden mountains in a previous life.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">He allows an exception to the Copy Principle for imaginative ideas.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The imagination can recombine simple ideas that each copy a real impression — the idea of gold and the idea of a mountain — into the complex idea of a golden mountain. The Copy Principle is preserved because imagination assembles but does not create from nothing.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Hume does not appeal to past lives, grant exceptions to the Copy Principle, or deny that we have the idea. His answer is that complex ideas are built from simple ones, each of which traces back to an impression. The imagination recombines but never invents entirely new content. Review the Impressions and Ideas lecture.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> <div><p>Hume identifies three principles by which ideas become associated in the mind. Which of the following correctly identifies all three?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Resemblance, contiguity, and cause and effect</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Identity, succession, and causation</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Similarity, memory, and emotional association</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Resemblance, contrast, and temporal sequence</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Hume identifies exactly three principles of association: resemblance (a portrait makes us think of the person), contiguity in time or place (mentioning one room makes us think of adjoining rooms), and cause and effect (thinking of a wound makes us think of the pain that follows).</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Contrast, identity, temporal sequence, and emotional association are not among Hume's three principles. The three are resemblance, contiguity, and cause and effect — and Hume treats these as the only natural relations connecting ideas in the mind. Review the Impressions and Ideas lecture.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> <div><p>When Hume describes the three laws of association, he is careful to say that these are laws governing the way the mind connects ideas — not laws about how the external world works. Why does this distinction matter?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">It means that Hume is only interested in psychology, not philosophy.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">It shows that the laws of association do not apply to emotional impressions, only cognitive ones.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">It leaves open the question of whether the connections the mind forms — especially causal connections — reflect real features of the world, which Hume will examine in Section 4.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">It establishes that the mind is independent of the body and therefore not subject to physical laws.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. By describing association as a 'gentle force' that habitually connects ideas, Hume deliberately leaves open whether these mental connections — especially causal ones — correspond to real connections in the world. This sets up his critical investigation of causation in Section 4.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The distinction matters not because Hume is doing psychology instead of philosophy, or because emotions are excluded. It matters because describing how the mind connects ideas is different from showing that those connections track reality — a question Hume takes up when he examines causation. Review the Impressions and Ideas lecture.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> <div><p>Hume's Fork divides all objects of human reason into "relations of ideas" and "matters of fact." Which of the following statements is true of <em>relations of ideas</em>?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">They are knowable through reason alone, and their contraries are inconceivable or self-contradictory.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Their contraries are conceivable but are known to be false through experiment.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">They include all claims about God, the soul, and the external world.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">They are discovered through careful observation and can be revised by experience.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Relations of ideas — such as mathematical and logical truths — are knowable by the operation of thought alone, and their denial involves a contradiction. This is one prong of Hume's Fork; the other prong, matters of fact, depends on experience and admits of conceivable contraries.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Relations of ideas are not discovered through observation, nor are their contraries merely unlikely — their contraries are inconceivable. Claims about God, the soul, and the external world fall under matters of fact, the other side of Hume's Fork. Review the Causation and Induction lecture.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> <div><p>Hume says that all reasoning about matters of fact is founded on the relation of cause and effect. Why does he then go on to argue that this relation itself cannot be established by reason?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">Because causal knowledge requires mathematical proof, and mathematics cannot be applied to empirical events.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Because Hume believes causation is an illusion and therefore not knowable by any means.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Because the senses are unreliable, and causal inference requires sensory data.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Because effects are always totally different from their causes — there is no necessary connection between them that reason can detect without prior experience.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Hume argues that no amount of pure reasoning can reveal what effect will follow from a given cause, because the effect is an entirely distinct event from the cause. Without prior experience, a person confronting a billiard ball collision for the first time could not deduce the outcome.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Hume's argument is not that the senses are unreliable or that causation is an illusion. His point is more precise: there is no logically necessary connection between any cause and its effect that reason alone could detect. Experience is always required. Review the Causation and Induction lecture.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> <div><p>Hume's problem of induction shows that we cannot rationally justify the belief that the future will resemble the past. What makes any attempted justification circular?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Because the future has not yet happened and therefore cannot provide evidence for any conclusion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Because any experiential justification ("it has worked before, so it will work again") is itself an inductive inference — it assumes the very principle it is meant to justify.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Because all justifications of induction rely on deductive reasoning, which is inapplicable to matters of fact.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Because induction relies on the senses, which Hume shows are fundamentally unreliable.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Any attempt to justify induction from experience is circular: 'the future will resemble the past because it always has' already assumes the uniformity of nature — the very principle it is supposed to establish. This is Hume's famous problem of induction.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The circularity is not about deduction being inapplicable or the future being unobservable. It is specifically about using past experience to justify reliance on past experience — which presupposes exactly what needs to be proved. Review the Causation and Induction lecture.</div>
  </div>
  
  
  
  
  


